## Proof

**Answer leak check:** The problem text contains only the statement and solving constraints. No leaked answers, solutions, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

**Key identity.** Note that
$$1 - 2n\cos x + n^2 = (1-ne^{ix})(1-ne^{-ix}) = |1-ne^{ix}|^2.$$
Since $\cos(2\pi-x)=\cos x$, the integrand $f(x)=\ln(1-2n\cos x+n^2)$ satisfies $f(2\pi-x)=f(x)$, hence
$$I(n)=\int_0^\pi f(x)\,dx = \frac{1}{2}\int_0^{2\pi} f(x)\,dx.$$

**Evaluating the full-period integral.** For $|n|<1$, using $\ln(1-ne^{ix})=-\sum_{k=1}^\infty \frac{n^k}{k}e^{ikx}$ (uniformly convergent), each term integrates to $0$ over $[0,2\pi]$, so $\int_0^{2\pi}\ln|1-ne^{ix}|^2\,dx=0$.

For $|n|>1$, write $|1-ne^{ix}|^2=n^2|1-n^{-1}e^{ix}|^2$, so
$$\int_0^{2\pi}\ln|1-ne^{ix}|^2\,dx = \int_0^{2\pi}2\ln|n|\,dx + \int_0^{2\pi}\ln|1-n^{-1}e^{ix}|^2\,dx = 4\pi\ln|n|+0.$$

By continuity the case $|n|=1$ also gives $0$. Therefore
$$I(n)=\begin{cases}0 & |n|\le 1,\\ 2\pi\ln|n| & |n|>1.\end{cases}$$

**Computing the expression.** Since $100,10,36,6>1$:
- $I(100)=2\pi\ln 100=2\pi\cdot 2\ln 10=4\pi\ln 10$,
- $I(10)=2\pi\ln 10$,
- $I(36)=2\pi\ln 36=2\pi\cdot 2\ln 6=4\pi\ln 6$,
- $I(6)=2\pi\ln 6$.

Thus
$$\frac{I(100)}{I(10)}=\frac{4\pi\ln 10}{2\pi\ln 10}=2,\qquad \frac{I(36)}{I(6)}=\frac{4\pi\ln 6}{2\pi\ln 6}=2.$$

$$\boxed{\frac{I(100)}{I(10)}\cdot\frac{I(36)}{I(6)}=2\cdot 2=4.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
